    {
      "id": "sol.amc-10a-2011-p16.live",
      "kind": "proof-sketch",
      "text": "Since we have square roots, we can try to change the inside of each radical to be a perfect square.\n\nNote that we can rewrite the expression as \\[ \\begin{aligned} & \\sqrt{6 - 6\\sqrt2 + 3} \\\\ &{}+ \\sqrt{6 + 6\\sqrt2 + 3}. \\end{aligned} \\]\n\nFactoring and simplifying gives us \\[ \\sqrt{(\\sqrt6 - \\sqrt3)^2} + \\sqrt{(\\sqrt6 + \\sqrt3)^2} \\] \\[ = \\sqrt6 - \\sqrt3 + \\sqrt6 + \\sqrt3 = 2\\sqrt6. \\]\n\nThus, B is the correct answer.",
      "hints": [
        "Square the whole expression",
        "The product \\((9-6\\sqrt2)(9+6\\sqrt2)\\) is small"
      ],
      "images": []
    }
  ],
